.SH "NAME"
activate-pam-session \- PAM module which opens and closes a PAM session
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.HP \w'\fBactivate-pam-session\fR\ 'u
\fBactivate-pam-session\fR 
[ \fB-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR ] |
[ \fB-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR ]
[ \fB-u\fR, \fB\-\-user\fR \fINAME\fR ]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
The \fBactivate-pam-session\fR tool authenticates a user, opens and closes a
PAM session. The goal of activating a PAM session is to trigger the \fBauth\fR
and \fBsession\fR stacks in \fI/etc/pam.d\fR.
If a user name is not provided, it uses the \fBUSER\fR environment variable.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
This tool recognizes the following options\&.
.TP
\fB-u\fR, \fB\-\-user\fR \fINAME\fR
specifies the use to authenticate as
.TP
\fB-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print usage informaiton and exit
.TP
\fB-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
enable extra runtime messages
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.PP
Returns \fB0\fR on success, otherwise \fB1\fR on any failure.
.RE
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.PP
Run the tool
.PP
.nf
activate-pam-session ---help
activate-pam-session --user siterep1
activate-pam-session -vu siterep1
.fi
.PP
Sample \fI/etc/pam.d/activate-pam-sesssion\fR file
.PP
.nf
auth        required    pam_rootok\&.so
session     required    system-auth
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBpam.conf\fR(5),
\fBpam.d\fR(5),
\fBpam\fR(8)
